  "query_preview": "..Scattered thunderstorms have developed over the past \ncouple of hours, with the main area of convection from Tucson \nsouthward. Most of the storms initially struggled with having \nsustained updrafts, but we've been seeing some better storm \norganization over the past hour or so, especially between Tucson and \nNogales. Main concern this afternoon into evening is locally heavy \nrainfall resulting in isolated flash flooding with a secondary \nthreat of gusty winds to around 50 mph. Storm movement today is slow \n(north-northeast about 5 to 10 mph) which will amplify the trend \ntowards those local areas of 1 to 2 inches of rain. While these \nstorms should diminish in scope during the evening hours, we'll \ncontinue to keep an eye towards Sonora after midnight into the early \nmorning for potential MCS. This could push additional outflow or \neven some overnight convection in our forecast area, especially \nsouth of Tucson. The HRRR has latched onto this solution but the \nother CAMs not as much, thus confidence is low but something we'll \nbe monitoring. Of course if late night convection were to occur or \nremnants of an MCS pushing through Thursday morning, then leftover \nclouds, subsidence or lower instability could reduce thunderstorm \nchances Thursday afternoon. \n\nOtherwise, more broadly speaking, we'll remain in an active pattern \nThursday/Friday and continuing into the weekend as lower level \nsoutherly flow keeps reinforcing moisture from Sonora. Additionally, \nthe southerly flow will get some aid from Tropical Storm Marie (well \nsouthwest of Baja) that will push additional moisture up the Gulf of \nCalifornia towards southern Arizona. This will keep an above average \nlate monsoon pattern for us at least through the weekend with \nprimary threat continuing to be heavy rain with an isolated to \nscattered flash flood threat. Note that the HREF shows this well for \nThursday with a 50% to 70% neighborhood probability (40-km) of 1\" or \nmore in 3 hours across Santa Cruz, Ern Pima, far SE Pinal and \nwestern Cochise Counties. There are no real well defined \ndisturbances/lift to really hone in on during this period for \nsoutheast Arizona, so we'll continue to see day-to-day variability \nin convection. Given the above normal moisture levels during the \nperiod (PWAT averaging 1.3 to 1.7 inches), there will be higher than \nnormal chances for nocturnal convection, but confidence is quite low \non timing. \n\nElevated moisture levels look to continue early next week as well, \nthough the flow pattern become a bit more southeasterly at that \ntime. \n\nTemperatures will be near or just slightly below normal through the \nforecast period.",
